// Data of the note on the paper "Playing Atari with Deep Reinforcement Learning" (Mnih et al. 2013), generated by
// course-materials/reinforcement-learning/scripts/deep-q-networks/build.mjs (outside the repository) from runs of deep
// Q-learning on the rally, the note's game. Do not edit by hand: rerun the script, which also rewrites the version in
// deep-q-networks.js's import of this file. Every number below was computed by the script; none is typed.
//
// THE GAME. A screen of 8 × 6 pixels; column x = 0 is the left wall, row y = 0 the top. The ball moves one pixel
// diagonally per step in columns 0–6 and bounces off the top, bottom and left walls. The paddle is 2 pixels tall in
// column 7; py is its top row (0–4), so it covers rows py and py + 1; it starts every serve on rows 2–3. Moves are
// numbered 0 up, 1 stay, 2 down, everywhere below. A hit (the ball arriving in column 6 on a row the paddle covers)
// gives r = +1 and turns the ball back; a miss gives r = −1 and ends the episode. A rally is cut after 10 hits (not an
// ending). A pair of screens φ is written as 6 small integers [pbx, pby, ppy, bx, by, py]: the earlier screen's ball
// column and row and paddle top row, then the later screen's; at a serve both screens are the same.
//
// THE RUN. Seed 48; plain gradient steps with α = 0.1; γ = 0.9; ε = 1 − 0.9·(t − 1)/10,000 at step t (1-based),
// 0.1 from step 10,001 on; a memory of the last 10,000 transitions; minibatches of 32 drawn uniformly with
// replacement; a network of 96 inputs, 32 rectifier units and 3 outputs (3,203 weights); 20,000 steps.
//
// UNITS AND ROUNDING. Values are integers in thousandths (1394 means 1.394), rounded half away from zero from the
// run's exact values. Figures 1 and 6 show two decimals: round their thousandths to hundredths half away from zero
// (h = sign(v) · floor((|v| + 5) / 10)); the script has adjusted the rare value whose third decimal is a 5 that the exact
// value does not reach, so that this always gives the exact value's two decimals. Figures 2 and 3 show three decimals:
// write the thousandths as they are. Ties: values equal at the shown precision count as tied, in Figures 1, 3 and 6
// (no blue arrow, the readout names the tie, and a target that borrows the largest value outlines every tied bar).
// Numbers keep the decimal point in both languages; format them with localNum.
//
// fig1 — Figure 1, the trained network over the states the drag can show (the paddle on rows 2–3 in both screens).
//   key[(bx·6 + by)·4 + (dx > 0 ? 2 : 0) + (dy > 0 ? 1 : 0)]: the entry (0–99) of the ball at column bx, row by,
//        moving dx (−1 left, +1 right) and dy (−1 rising, +1 falling); −1 where the drag never goes (a serve, a ball
//        moving right at column 0; rising at row 5 or falling at row 0; moving left at column 5). The earlier screen's
//        ball is at (bx − dx, by − dy). Default: the ball at (5, 2) rising from (4, 3), entry key[(5·6 + 2)·4 + 2].
//   two[e]  [up, stay, down]: the two-screen network's values Q(φ, a; θ) at step 20,000, in thousandths (2 decimals).
//   star[e] [up, stay, down]: the exact values Q*(s, a) of the true state (the red lines), in thousandths (2 decimals).
//   k[e]    steps until the ball arrives beside the paddle's column (1: next step). The readout's kinds: exact values
//           all equal and negative: too late; all equal and positive: far or moving away; else k = 1: one step away,
//           k = 2 or 3: the ball arrives in k steps.
//   one[bx·6 + by] [up, stay, down]: the one-screen network's values (it sees only the later screen), in thousandths
//           (2 decimals), for the ball at (bx, by), bx and by 0–5.
//   alias   [[bx, by], …]: the positions where no move is right for both directions of a ball moving right.
//
// fig2 — Figure 2, the run's first transition learned alone from the starting weights.
//   phi     [φ_1, φ_2] as pairs of screens; a: the move (stay), r: the reward (0).
//   net, held: the run with the target from the network (recomputed before every step) and with the target held.
//     q[i]  Q(φ_1, stay; θ_i), i = 0–30, in thousandths. The value is the blue line, from i = 0.
//     y[i]  y_i, the target of the step from θ_{i−1} to θ_i, i = 1–30 (y[0] is null), in thousandths. The red line,
//           from i = 1. Held: every y[i] is y[1].
//     eq    the readout's calculation y_i ≈ r + 0.9 · m ≈ y_i: { i, m }, the iteration (30 from the network, 1 held)
//           and m, the largest value of φ_2 from θ_{i−1}, in thousandths; m4, present only where m's three decimals
//           would not round to y_i's three decimals, gives m in ten-thousandths, to be shown with four decimals.
//
// fig3 — Figure 3, the guided run.
//   steps[t − 1], t = 1–12: episode 1 in full and episode 2 to its first hit, one entry per step of the run (the
//           transition stored at step t is transition t, the number of its memory cell).
//     ep    the episode. s: φ_t, the pair of screens in view when the move is chosen. s1: [bx, by, py], the screen after
//           the move; after a miss, the ball beside the paddle's column. q: Q(φ_t, ·; θ) when choosing. a: the move.
//           rnd: 1 if the move was random (ε-greedy's random branch). r: the reward (−1 ends the episode).
//     qn, qa: Q(φ_{t+1}, ·; θ), the pair in view after the move, before and after the step's update (null after a
//           miss: "end" replaces the bars).
//     draws the 32 transitions the minibatch drew, in order (each a transition number; the count of t in draws is how
//           often the newest was drawn, "×14").
//     v0, v1: the newest transition's value Q(φ_t, a_t; θ) before and after the update; y: its target, r + 0.9·m, or r
//           after a miss; m: the largest value of φ_{t+1} before the update (the bars outlined red are those equal to
//           it at three decimals; null after a miss); m4: m in ten-thousandths, present only where m's three decimals
//           would not round to y's (show four decimals then); pct: the share of the way to the target the update
//           moved it, in percent; life: how often that transition is drawn over the whole run.
//   landings[i]: the episodes the skips land on, for slides 5, 6 and 7.
//     slide, ep, from: the slide, the episode and the run's step of its first move.
//     frames[n]: step from + n, as [pbx, pby, ppy, bx, by, py, q_up, q_stay, q_down, a, rnd, r]: the pair in view when
//           the move is chosen, its values in thousandths, the move, random or not, the reward.
//     end   the pair of screens after the last move, and, when the episode goes on (a cut, or the run's end), its
//           three values after that step's update; after a miss, the six integers alone.
//   stops[i]: the memory at each slide's stop (the step of the landing episode's last move).
//     slide, t, ep: the slide, the run's step and the episode. size: transitions in the memory (min(t, 10,000)); the
//           strip shows them oldest at the left, transition t − size + 1 in slot 0 and transition t in slot size − 1.
//     draws the 32 transitions drawn at step t (transition j sits in slot j − (t − size + 1)).
//     slide 5: window [first, last step], decisive (screens one step from the paddle where the move decided a hit),
//           right (of them, those where the network valued a hitting move most); check: the greedy check at step
//           5,000, { t, perfect, missed, certain } (serves played perfectly, missed, missed with the miss certain
//           before the last move).
//     slide 6: age (the 32 draws' mean age in steps, rounded), differ (draws holding a move the network would not
//           choose now), random (draws holding a random move).
//     slide 7: full (the step from which the memory is full), stable (every check is perfect from this step on).
//
// fig4 — Figure 4, 16 agents (seeds 1–16) each of Replay, Last 32 and Latest only, α = 0.1, 30,000 steps.
//   every   250: the checks are at steps 0, 250, …, 30,000 (121 checks).
//   replay, last32, latest: at each check, how many of the 16 agents played all 12 serves perfectly (10 hits each).
//   sum.{replay, last32, latest}: reached (agents with a perfect check by step 30,000), median (the median of those
//           agents' first perfect checks, the lower of the two middle ones; Replay and Last 32: all 16 got there),
//           later (of each such agent's checks from its first perfect one on, the percent that miss a serve, averaged
//           over those agents, rounded), allFirst (the first check at which all 16 are perfect, or null), atEnd
//           (agents perfect at step 30,000).
//
// fig5 — Figure 5, the run's checks every 250 steps (every = 250), steps 0–20,000 (81 checks).
//   checks[i], at step 250·i: [total reward per episode over 10,000 steps of ε = 0.05 play on the evaluation's own
//           stream, in hundredths (rounded from the exact ratio); the average largest value over the 500 fixed pairs
//           of screens, in thousandths; serves greedy play played perfectly, of 12].
//
// fig6 — Figure 6, the final network's ε = 0.05 play on mulberry32(777 + 48): the last 40 steps of episode 6.
//   ep, from: the episode and the step of the rally of the first entry (steps from … from + 39).
//   prev    the network's largest value at step from − 1, in thousandths (2 decimals): the step before the stretch.
//   steps[n]: step from + n, as [pbx, pby, ppy, bx, by, py, q_up, q_stay, q_down, v, k, a, rnd, r]: the pair of screens,
//           the network's values and v = max_a Q*(s, a), the exact largest value (the red dots), all in thousandths
//           (2 decimals); k, the steps until the ball arrives beside the paddle's column; the move, random or not; the
//           reward (+1 a hit, −1 the miss).
